    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Of course, a combination of Lemma 3.1, Lemma 3.2, and Lemma 3.5 completes the proof of Theorem 1.1."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 44,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.71, 317.668, 485.506, 383.273 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "It remains to prove Proposition 1.2. Recall that the composition series (1.1) is called stratiﬁed if for any irreducible representation π of B with π(Jk) = {0} we have π(Jk+1) = π(Jk) [13, Deﬁnition 0.3]. Moreover, a solving, stratiﬁed composition series coincides with the maximal radical series by [13, Proposition 0.4]. If we abbreviate := lengthQB(Z), then the series"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 45,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.712, 296.856, 388.383, 316.074 ],
    "font" : "CMSY10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "(3.1) QB(Z) ⊇ J +1 ⊇ J ⊇ ··· ⊇ J1 = {0}"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 46,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.707, 236.129, 485.517, 296.154 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "with Jk given by (2.3) is solving of minimal length by Theorem 1.1 and the proof of Lemma 3.2. Using the description (2.4) of the irreducible representations of QB(Z) and the compatibility conditions (2.1) and (2.2), it is straightforward to check that the series (3.1) is stratiﬁed. Thus, it is the maximal radical series for QB(Z), hence, an application of Lemma 3.1 completes the proof of Proposition 1.2."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 47,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 261.593, 206.728, 350.531, 216.691 ],
